  "domain": "infrastructure",
  "definition": "An Application-Specific Integrated Circuit (ASIC) is a custom integrated circuit designed and fabricated to perform a specific function or narrow set of functions with maximum efficiency, in contrast to general-purpose processors such as CPUs and GPUs that are optimised for programmable flexibility. ASICs achieve superior performance-per-watt and cost efficiency at volume compared to programmable alternatives by eliminating logic that would support unused instruction sets or configurations. They are central to cryptocurrency mining, AI inference acceleration, networking hardware, and consumer electronics where power, cost, and performance constraints demand purpose-built silicon.",
